INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE TESTING CHAT.  Pass on everything from this line down
to the diffstat.

Goal: show what this one-line kernel change does on real FUSE servers, and
that it breaks nothing in FUSE.

This patch changes the kernel.  Build TWO kernels from the same tree and
the same .config: K0 without this patch, K1 with it.  Nothing else may
differ.  Use the config from the selftest patch
(selftests-mm-check-that-fsync-reports-hard-poisoned-dirty-page-cache)
plus FUSE_DAX, PROVE_LOCKING, DEBUG_VM and DEBUG_LIST.

Test binary: tools/testing/selftests/mm/memory-failure built from a tree
with that selftest patch applied, but with its one ftruncate(fd, 0) line
and the comment above it deleted.  Call it B-notrunc.  That line hides
the problem this patch fixes, so it must not be there.  Say in the report
that you removed it.

Part 1, on K0 and then on K1.  For each FUSE setup below, cd into a fresh
directory on it and run B-notrunc 40 times.

  fuse         a libfuse passthrough example over ext4
  fuse         the same with the writeback cache enabled
  virtiofs     virtiofsd with its default cache mode
  virtiofs     with writeback enabled
  virtiofs     with DAX, if the host can provide it
  overlayfs    upper and lower on virtiofs, if it will mount
  ext4         control, 5 runs: must be identical on K0 and K1

Record for each: passes out of 40, every distinct failing assertion with
its count, and from the kernel log the counts of

  recovery action for dirty LRU page: Recovered / Failed / Delayed
  recovery action for clean LRU page: Recovered / Failed / Delayed
  Failed to invalidate

Expected on K0: "Failed to invalidate" for every hard dirty_pagecache
run, and some runs failing in teardown with

  memory-failure.c:211:...:Expected unpoison_memory(self->pfn) (-16) == 0

Expected on K1: no "Failed to invalidate", dirty LRU page Recovered,
40 of 40.

Part 2, on K0 and then on K1: regressions.  On the fuse passthrough mount
and on virtiofs run whatever FUSE coverage the test machine has, in this
order of preference: fstests "-g quick" with FSTYP=fuse or virtiofs, the
libfuse test suite, otherwise fsx and fsstress for 10 minutes each.
Report the list of failing tests on K0 and on K1.  Only differences
between the two lists matter.

Part 3, on K1 only: poison under load.  On the fuse passthrough mount
with the writeback cache enabled, run fsstress or fsx on one file while a
second process repeatedly mmaps that file MAP_SHARED, dirties a random
page and calls madvise(MADV_HWPOISON) on it, for 5 minutes.  Unpoison
through /sys/kernel/debug/hwpoison/unpoison-pfn afterwards.  Report any
WARNING, BUG, lockdep report, hung task or bad page state in the kernel
log.  I/O errors and SIGBUS in the test programs are expected.

Rules:

- A failure is a result.  Report it.  Do not change the kernel, the test
  or the mount options to make something pass.
- If a setup cannot be made or mounted, report it as NOT RUN with the
  exact command and error.  Do not leave it out.
- Any WARNING, BUG or lockdep report in the kernel log on K1 which is not
  also on K0 must be quoted in full.
- Check HardwareCorrupted in /proc/meminfo before Part 1 and after
  Part 3.  Report both values.

Report: one table for Part 1 with K0 and K1 side by side, the two lists
for Part 2, the kernel log findings for Part 3, then anything NOT RUN.

 fs/fuse/file.c |    1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/fs/fuse/file.c~fuse-let-memory-failure-remove-poisoned-page-cache-folios
+++ a/fs/fuse/file.c
@@ -3156,6 +3156,7 @@ static const struct address_space_operat
 	.readahead	= fuse_readahead,
 	.writepages	= fuse_writepages,
 	.launder_folio	= fuse_launder_folio,
+	.error_remove_folio = generic_error_remove_folio,
 	.dirty_folio	= iomap_dirty_folio,
 	.release_folio	= iomap_release_folio,
 	.invalidate_folio = iomap_invalidate_folio,
_
